// excluded so an escape and an interpolation are reached first.
MlPlain     = !'"""' ( "\x00"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ) ;

// Raw string literals: #"..."# and ##"..."##. Nothing inside is an escape - a
// backslash is a backslash - and the only interpolation is \#( ... ) / \##( ... ),
// whose pound count matches the delimiter's. Two pound levels are supported, which
// is what real code uses; more would just be more of the same rules.
// Three pound levels. The multi-line raw forms (###""" ... """###) ride the same
// rules: the opening delimiter starts with ###" and the closing one ends with "###,
// so the extra quotes are simply content - which is exactly the text Swift keeps.
// Raw string delimiters BEYOND three pounds - `#####"This is a string"#####`.
// The levels 1..3 above are spelled as ordinary rules because they support the
// matching `\#( ... )` interpolation, which needs a real Expression sub-parse; a
// scanner cannot call the parser back. Level 4 and up is a `:script` that counts
// the pounds and returns the whole literal as ONE token, so the level is unbounded.
// Two shapes are deliberately DECLINED rather than mis-read, and each stays the
// parse failure it already was: a MULTI-LINE raw literal (`####""" ... """####`),
// whose closing-delimiter indentation strip lives in makeMultiStr, and any literal
// carrying its own `\####(` interpolation.
